                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "This is kind of a mix between beat 'em up and shoot 'em up with an original art style I've not seen anywhere else before. It's that and the humor obvious in the trailer that attracted me in the first place. The art has nice, vibrant colors and is initially attractive, but it doesn't hold up in the long run because the drawings are too simplistic and the backgrounds are too repetitive. The humor is good, I liked how they made the writing expressive with effects and fonts, but it didn't make me laugh. The music eventually turns out to be disappointing and repetitive. While playing, the heroes and enemies almost always run, which is different, and the fighting can be fun. However, you realize at one point that the dash move is too useful compared to the other moves and you end up using mostly that.\n\nYou can buy weapons of the melee and thrown varieties and, I can't believe I'm writing this, there's too many of them. You can find skins that appeal, but the rapidity of unlocks is overwhelming. In Remote Play coop mode, there's chaotic entertainment to be had, but because of special effects and shaking, you often end up losing sight of where you are. It otherwise works well enough. In the last two \"regions\", it got so tough, that we had to equip an amulet that reduced the damage by half. And we're not amateur gamers so this means the game gets difficult. The achievements have been broken for years and I doubt they will ever be fixed. I wish Ninjin had been better, but I still had a good enough time to recommend.",
